1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,820
One lottery jackpot almost buys a penny tower to the Moon.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,400
Okay, let's do the math, nice and slow.

3
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:10,640
A penny is about one and a half millimeters thick.

4
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:14,860
The Moon? About two hundred thirty-nine thousand miles away.

5
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:19,420
Pretend the stack is perfectly straight and never falls. Pure fantasy.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,340
Tap the heart if you've ever knocked over a coin stack.

7
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:26,820
To reach the Moon, you'd need about two hundred fifty-three billion pennies.

8
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:29,680
That's thirty-one pennies for every human on Earth.

9
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:31,480
A hundred pennies make a dollar.

10
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:34,920
So the tower costs about two and a half billion dollars.

11
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:38,880
Now, the biggest lottery jackpot ever? About two billion dollars.

12
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:41,780
So one lucky ticket gets you most of the way there.

13
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:44,460
Send this to the friend who throws pennies in the trash.

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,180
Oh, and it would weigh about six hundred thirty thousand tons.

15
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:51,300
But watch what happens when we swap pennies for dollar bills.

16
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:54,360
Bills are paper thin, so you need way more.

17
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:59,000
A tower of dollar bills to the Moon costs about thirty-five trillion dollars.

18
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:01,800
Follow if you already knew this would get ugly.

19
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:04,820
That's roughly seventeen thousand record jackpots.

20
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:08,840
So the most useless coin in your pocket is the cheapest ladder to space.

21
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:12,380
Pennies or bills, which tower are you funding? One word.

